  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/bb96a7692787ca6e055b41d4f1599b87baa1ea331544ea42235a7131343c76e3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?",
  "worked_solution": "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/54735541d4dfc424fe186ddabe0c3f9d3cbd45a0dc7a1b8f4551cf8c5e5bc14f.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/f00f882c97fd201178e54f29151a48a4f41d4108cf375104cc338e968c55761b.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/6e0c0320775d93364e32655d1e09081382078fa7e96fbe621a8c558f63dfcabb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
